---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeBinary from '@/components/tools/text/DevEncodeBinary.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="text-to-binary">
  <DevEncodeBinary client:load kind="text" direction="encode" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How text to binary conversion works</h2>
    <p>
      Each character has a numeric code. The code is written in base 2 (ones and zeros) and padded to a fixed
      width, so a reader knows where one character ends and the next starts. With 8-bit groups, the letter
      <code>A</code> (code 65) becomes <code>01000001</code>.
    </p>
    <pre><code>H  →  72  →  01001000
e  → 101  →  01100101
l  → 108  →  01101100
l  → 108  →  01101100
o  → 111  →  01101111</code></pre>

    <h2>Reading a byte</h2>
    <p>
      The place values double from the right: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128. Add the columns that hold a 1 to get
      the character code.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>128</th><th>64</th><th>32</th><th>16</th><th>8</th><th>4</th><th>2</th><th>1</th><th>Total</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>64 + 8 = 72 = <code>H</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>64+32+4+1 = 101 = <code>e</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>7-bit or 8-bit?</h2>
    <p>
      Original ASCII was a 7-bit code with 128 characters, enough for English and little else. The eighth bit
      of a byte was a parity bit for error checking on serial lines. When memory became cheaper, the parity
      bit was used to extend the character set, and 8 bits became the norm.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Choose <strong>8-bit</strong> when the binary represents bytes as a computer stores them.</li>
      <li>Choose <strong>7-bit</strong> for classroom exercises that follow the original ASCII table.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For characters below 128 the two differ only by a leading zero, but a decoder set to the wrong width gives nonsense.</p>

    <h2>Unicode makes strings longer than they look</h2>
    <p>
      In UTF-8, only plain ASCII is one byte. Other characters take more, and the binary shows it:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Character</th><th>UTF-8 bytes</th><th>Bits</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>A</code></td><td>1</td><td>8</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>é</code></td><td>2</td><td>16</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>字</code></td><td>3</td><td>24</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>😀</code></td><td>4</td><td>32</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>👨‍👩‍👧</code></td><td>18</td><td>144</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The last row is a family emoji: three people joined by two zero-width joiners. It is one
      &ldquo;character&rdquo; on screen and eighteen bytes on disk, so character limits and byte limits are
      different.
    </p>

    <h2>Binary hides nothing</h2>
    <p>
      Binary is not encryption. It is the same information in base 2, and anyone can reverse it in a second,
      for example with the binary-to-text tool on this site. Use it to look at data, not to protect it.
    </p>

    <h2>Translating a file</h2>
    <p>
      Drop a text file to convert its first 64&nbsp;KB. Binary is eight characters per byte, so a megabyte of
      input would give eight million characters: more than a browser shows comfortably or an editor accepts
      as a paste.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
